ts)|unverified=not merged: draft PR #155 OPEN at head 30a08260d9827d61b37d8fbb4817513f9e487369 (MEASURED with gh in the report step); the same literature gaps as A2; unextracted parts of G1D (archive, verifier code) not read; no Japanese, Chinese or proceedings search for certified sector counts in finite-group gauge models|novelty_kind=new theorem about a particular model (conditional)|importance=supplies the isolation prerequisite named in issue #137 (E3 separated from the even pair, odd sector empty below z0); certifies no spectral weight; finite, fixed-volume and floor-conditional|json=0
PRIORITY|candidate=A3a-technique #137 per-reflection-sector residual Schur inertia counting by exact integer congruence (y137_k3_sector_isolation.py docstring, items 4 and the sandwich paragraph)|class=PRIOR|label=PRIORITY_UNVERIFIED|closest=Boyd and Vandenberghe 2004 eq. (A.14), VERIFIED; Umeno 23668824 Sec. 3.2 'Full-output Gram and the Schur comparison', VERIFIED; Umeno 23641690 Thm 3.1 (the third window split into charge-conjugation-even and -odd blocks), VERIFIED; Haynsworth 1968 and Sylvester's law of inertia, inertia additivity over direct sums, classical, UNVERIFIED primaries; Vehale and Goel 2026, symveig, arXiv:2606.16217, VERIFIED abstract only|comparison=The reflection R commutes with H and preserves P (it permutes flats 0..5,7,6) and Q, so K_z, S(z), U and LOWER commute with R and split into even and odd blocks by congruence; the inertia of a direct sum is the sum of the inertias, and the A2-technique sandwich holds blockwise. Per-sector certified counting is G1D's procedure (hypotheses: a symmetry acting reducibly, a sector-wise complement floor d_E - t > 0; conclusion: certified counts per sector below t). Lane A2 found no mathematical refinement beyond A2-technique's and called the exact integer decoupling check 'an implementation detail'|contrary=Against the PRIOR class (preserved, from A-hunter): as for A2-technique, PRIOR rests on composing classical facts with an arbitrary-X sandwich that no single opened source states; an adjudicator may keep lane A2's PARTIALLY_PRIOR. Against originality: G1D and G1B; symmetry block-diagonalization before an eigenvalue count is standard numerical practice (G1D treats all 20 O_h x C sectors); the Umeno Lehmann-Maehly addendum (23789573) gives sector lower bounds by another classical method|unverified=symmetry-adapted verified-eigenvalue monographs not opened; the exact integer sector-congruence procedure not searched in the computer-algebra literature|novelty_kind=none at the level of mathematics; the exact integer decoupling check is implementation|importance=method-level only; enables A3a; does not enlarge scope|json=0
PRIORITY|candidate=A3b #137 proposed strictly positive r=9 first-excitation weight abs(<psi_2, O+ psi_1>_W)^2 > 0 for the seven-loop standard-character Wilson sum, with the filter criterion norm(P_first v)_W^2 >= (L - b^2 U)/(a^2 - b^2) > 0 when L > b^2 U (issue #137 body and comment; README: interacting Wilson first-state weight status OPEN)|class=PARTIALLY_PRIOR|label=PRIORITY_UNVERIFIED|closest=L. Lin and Y. Tong 2020, Optimal polynomial based quantum eigenstate filtering with application to solving quantum linear systems, Quantum 4, 361, arXiv:1910.14596v4, Lemma 2 (norm(R_l(H~,Delta~) - P_lambda) <= 2e^(-sqrt2 l Delta~)) and Thm 3, VERIFIED (primary PDF); Anderson and Weinhold 1974, Dipole oscillator-strengths, with rigorous limits of error, for He and Li, Phys. Rev. A 9, 118-128, doi:10.1103/PhysRevA.9.118, UNVERIFIED (bibliographic and abstract records); Weinhold 1968-1972 (Adv. Quantum Chem. 6; Int. J. Quantum Chem. 1969), UNVERIFIED (snippets); Davis and Kahan 1970, The rotation of eigenvectors by a perturbation III, SIAM J. Numer. Anal. 7, 1-46, UNVERIFIED; Kato 1949 and Temple 1928, UNVERIFIED; Umeno G1A, G1B, G1D and G1D-LM, VERIFIED (text search found no transition-element or spectral-weight certificate; a negative within those four texts only)|comparison=The criterion follows in one line from the spectral theorem: norm(Fv)^2 <= a^2 norm(P_1 v)^2 + b^2 (norm(v)^2 - norm(P_1 v)^2). Lin-Tong: a polynomial filter equal to 1 on the target eigenvalue and bounded by b elsewhere satisfies norm(f(H) - P_lambda) <= b, the a=1 case. Anderson-Weinhold (approximate wavefunctions, rigorous energy bounds, Eckart/Weinhold overlap bounds, giving guaranteed intervals for transition moments) and Davis-Kahan (residual plus certified gap, giving sin theta <= norm(r)/gap) certify transition elements and overlaps. So the mechanism is known. Possibly new: the application to the seven-loop S3 Wilson sum in the W-weighted 3x4 orbit space at r=9, using the certified #136 and #137 windows to control E1 leakage and E3..E8 contamination. No opened source treats a gauge-invariant Wilson observable's first-excitation weight with certified bounds in any finite-group or SU(3) Hamiltonian lattice model. The weight itself has not been produced (OPEN): only the criterion is classified, and there is no result yet whose priority could be decided|contrary=Lin-Tong 2020 states the filter-to-projector bound (count 0 in repo markdown); GPT-6's #137 comment: diagonal flat-holonomy characters, flat Wilson characters included, have exactly zero matrix element between the leading-order C1 ground mode (classes 0,1,2) and first mode (classes 3,4), so any positive weight must come from higher-order mixing (feasibility, not priority); internal precedents: #147 certified a squared first-state weight > 1/256 for a different nonlocal flat-projected dual-electric operator at r >= 100000, and #141 certified a strictly zero element for O_alt; reflection positivity (Osterwalder-Seiler 1978, Ann. Phys. 110, 440-471, UNVERIFIED) gives nonnegative Euclidean spectral weights, not strict positivity for a named state|unverified=the idea has not been executed: no filter f(H), no rational L and U; Anderson-Weinhold, Weinhold, Eckart 1930 and Davis-Kahan primaries not opened; no deep lattice-QCD search for rigorous lower bounds on glueball-operator overlaps; no matrix-analysis search for this exact filter projector bound (elementary, very likely stated somewhere)|novelty_kind=none at the method level (Lin-Tong 2020); possibly new application to a particular model, not yet executed|importance=if certified, one specific gauge-invariant observable detects E2 in this finite model; no particle mass, volume-uniform gap or continuum statement|json=0
